          "tool_use_id": "toolu_019Bto35p3hYcXNzuhh42fsb",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022concepts/log-metric-correlation.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Log/Metric Correlation\\ndescription: Correlating log records and metrics through propagated trace context to enable observability pivoting.\\ntimestamp: 2026-01-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Observability\\n  - concept/Log-Metric-Correlation\\n  - tech/OpenTelemetry\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022Single authoritative source (OpenTelemetry); describes mechanism clearly but lacks supplementary corroboration.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\nLog/metric correlation is an observability technique that links log records and metric measurements through shared trace context, enabling operators to navigate from aggregate signals (like a metric spike) down to the specific events and causally-linked execution paths that produced them.\\n\\n## Mechanism\\n\\nCorrelation works by propagating **trace_id** and **span_id** context into:\\n\\n- **Log records**: Each log entry carries the trace and span identifiers from the active execution context, making it possible to filter logs by trace.\\n- **Metric exemplars**: Numeric metric points can be annotated with trace/span context, anchoring a measurement to the specific trace that produced it.\\n\\nThis allows an operator to:\\n\\n1. Observe a spike or anomaly in a metric\\n2. Pivot to the exemplar on that metric point\\n3. Follow the trace_id into distributed traces to see the causally-linked spans\\n4. Cross-reference the same trace_id in logs to see structured events from the same execution\\n\\n## Distinction from Signal Types\\n\\nLog/metric correlation is not simply \\u0022having logs and metrics\\u0022 \\u2014 it is the **active linking** of those signals through trace context. This differs from the three signal types themselves (traces, metrics, logs), which describe what kinds of data are collected. Correlation is about connecting them.\\n\\n## Citations\\n\\n[^1]: OpenTelemetry specification on traces, metrics, and logs signal types and their correlation mechanics.\\n\u0022}"
        }
